WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.360
Interaction is a well-trodden question within&nbsp;
the EDH community with raging debates over how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.560
much is too much, how little is too little, what&nbsp;
types are the best, and what types are the rudest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.960
I’ve thrown my hat in this ring before, but what&nbsp;
has struck me recently is how many people seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.080
to not really understand interaction as a&nbsp;
concept, and what it is accomplishing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.120
any given deck. As with many subjects, we as&nbsp;
the EDH community have created lots of little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.320
myths about interaction and removal. When I say&nbsp;
myths, I don’t mean strictly untrue statements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.520
but rather kinda true things accepted as&nbsp;
undeniable fact, or sometimes-true things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.960
overgeneralized to always be true. So, I wanna&nbsp;
tackle some of these misunderstandings, and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:47.440
the process drill into the fundamentals of what&nbsp;
interaction does, why, and how best to use it.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.120
To start with, let’s talk about what’s&nbsp;
worth considering alongside your removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.920
when deckbuilding. I’ve occasionally gotten some&nbsp;
flak because I tend to have a pretty wide scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.400
of what I consider interaction and removal, and&nbsp;
I’ll sometimes organize my interaction suites in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.280
ways that other people find unintuitive.&nbsp;
Graveyard exiling is a form of removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.920
bounce spells are a form of removal, and defensive&nbsp;
cards like propaganda are a form of removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160
though the degree this label will fit can&nbsp;
depend on the deck. I think the reason this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.840
rubs people wrong is because they have a very&nbsp;
specific idea of what removal entails—removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.840
is when you remove an opposing thing, and&nbsp;
more specific tools like defensive cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.480
graveyard hate, protection, and pinpoint land&nbsp;
destruction deserve to be placed in an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.680
different mental category, their own boxes on&nbsp;
a checklist. In some decks these things will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.560
warrant this sort of separation, but in many&nbsp;
others doing this is a waste of time at best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.760
and in the worst cases actively counterproductive&nbsp;
to constructing a functional deck. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.520
instead of this sort of checklist approach,&nbsp;
the best way to think about interaction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.240
as a network of overlapping solutions&nbsp;
to a number of specific gamestates.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.640
Let’s say an opponent plays Animate Dead to bring&nbsp;
back an Archon of Cruelty, and let’s say this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.600
poses a problem to us—how can we shut this down&nbsp;
before they start attacking us with it? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.640
we could destroy the Archon of Cruelty&nbsp;
with creature removal, we could destroy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.960
Animate Dead with enchantment removal, we could&nbsp;
counter the Animate Dead with a counterspell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.840
and we could use instant speed graveyard hate to&nbsp;
exile the Archon of Cruelty in response. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.240
some less common approaches as well, but let’s&nbsp;
stick with these four. In this case, all four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.480
of these options would be ways of removing this&nbsp;
threat, dealing with this gamestate that I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.360
decided is problematic to us. I’ll allow myself a&nbsp;
single cheeky comment here and point out that, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.440
our purposes here, Abrade is not a removal spell,&nbsp;
and Cremate is. For another example, let’s say our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.000
opponent is playing a Krenko deck, and is starting&nbsp;
to pop off. Several goblins, a couple sources of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.760
untapping, an Anger in the graveyard, the works. A&nbsp;
piece of instant speed creature removal could deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.440
with this, so long as the Krenko isn’t wearing&nbsp;
a Swiftfoot Boots or something. However, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.200
are also a number of less absolute ways to slow&nbsp;
down or survive this sort of play—you could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.040
a Soul Sister out to allow your life total to keep&nbsp;
pace with the number of goblins on board, or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.200
could have a defensive card like a Ghostly Prison&nbsp;
already in play to limit the number of attackers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.392
You could even have a Thalia, Heretic Cathar to&nbsp;
have the goblins enter tapped, or have a Rakdos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.392 --> 00:03:08.080
Charm at the ready to explode your opponent’s&nbsp;
life total as punishment for their token crimes.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.800
Now what point am I trying to make here,&nbsp;
you might ask? Put simply, in EDH problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.040
are often more complicated than one single&nbsp;
problem permanent that needs to be removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.520
and even more complicated than just being the&nbsp;
occasional spooky board that needs wiping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.240
Single target removal and generic board wipes&nbsp;
can be the solution to a lot of problems, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.640
I’d certainly never tell you to avoid these cards,&nbsp;
but they’re far from the only options available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.880
and a lot of times they won’t be the most&nbsp;
efficient way to tackle a given problem. Sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.600
of graveyard exile are generally extremely cheap&nbsp;
per mana, as are stax cards, defensive permanents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.920
and other things that stick around to hinder&nbsp;
multiple opposing plays. As a result, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.600
beneficial to get to know these types of cards,&nbsp;
and the sorts of decks and metagames where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.840
could play the role of more traditional removal&nbsp;
better. When newer players are being inducted into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.720
the ways of the commander format, discussions of&nbsp;
interaction tend to center card types as a thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.680
to focus on—you want a way to hit creatures,&nbsp;
to hit artifacts, to hit enchantments, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.360
on. To be clear, I have no problem with this—card&nbsp;
types are undoubtedly a good thing to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:12.560
as a starting point. However, as you get more&nbsp;
acquainted with common play patterns of decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.200
and which ones you tend to have a problem with,&nbsp;
it becomes much more useful to focus on situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.960
that need to be remedied. Some decks won’t care&nbsp;
about certain types of threats as much—a go-wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.360
tokens deck isn’t going to be as spooky if you’re&nbsp;
playing a thick-boarded midrange deck, a pinging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.880
engine isn’t going to be as spooky if you’re&nbsp;
playing a deck with a good amount of lifegain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.200
decks with more explosive plays aren’t going to&nbsp;
be as spooky if you’re already packing a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.520
of hate pieces, and so on. If you’re playing&nbsp;
a deck with a focus on some defensive cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.680
you’re already going to be somewhat shielded from&nbsp;
a lot of plays that involve large combat steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.560
and if you’re playing a deck that runs&nbsp;
some counterspells for protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.200
you’re already going to have some ways to nail&nbsp;
critical centerpiece cards or combos. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.360
an interaction suite that does a good job&nbsp;
handling the threats of one table might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.680
do as good a job at a different table—you need&nbsp;
to know the environment you’re stepping into.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.040
To clarify, if what I’m saying sounds&nbsp;
overwhelming, you shouldn’t worry about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.920
too much. What I’m describing is what takes place&nbsp;
during a potentially months-long process of tuning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.920
and pruning a deck, not necessarily what you&nbsp;
should be doing when you’re first putting together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.880
a deck. It’s fine if you’re using templates or&nbsp;
rough rules of thumb to start out with, but as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.080
you get deeper into tuning an individual deck,&nbsp;
and as you get more experienced as a deckbuilder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.800
it’s generally good to move away from deciding&nbsp;
counts of different cards using a simple template.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.200
There’s no way to know exactly how many removal&nbsp;
spells or overall interaction spells you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.120
because different forms of removal will have&nbsp;
overlap in the profile of what they can answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.120
and different interaction spells will have&nbsp;
different degrees in how much they hinder opposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:47.280
plays. Some of my decks run interaction counts in&nbsp;
the teens, but others might include 20-something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.600
or even 30-something cards that interact&nbsp;
with the resources of opponents in some way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.440
excluding life total. And, excluding life total&nbsp;
isn’t even necessarily a fair thing to do. I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.680
know if you’ve ever watched how a person with 14&nbsp;
life conducts themself within a commander game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.160
but it’s generally very different than how&nbsp;
somebody with 34 life conducts themself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.480
That 14 life total person will often find themself&nbsp;
holding back on attacks and chump-blocking more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.840
often than they’d like, and they’re also often&nbsp;
going to be more conservative in the sorts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.320
spells they play. Tapping out to draw a bunch of&nbsp;
cards or play some powerful amplifier permanent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.400
is perfectly commonplace for somebody at&nbsp;
a higher life total, but it’s a terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.600
proposition when finding just a little bit of&nbsp;
extra damage could let an opponent swing lethal.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.040
For that reason, having a higher degree of&nbsp;
life total pressure can often allow for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.200
bit smaller of a removal count. All sorts of&nbsp;
late game permanents that would generally be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.280
good candidates for removal become a lot less&nbsp;
of a concern if their controller is in range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.840
being rapidly focused down. This is one reason&nbsp;
why Magister Sphinx is such a useful card in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.560
Sharuum Slide deck—the deck generally looks to&nbsp;
build toward a late-game win, so having a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.120
put an opponent on death’s door if they’re pushing&nbsp;
out of control a bit too early is a sweet escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.040
valve that takes a little bit of pressure off the&nbsp;
deck’s suite of control spells. Overall, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.440
this life total rule is going to be most pertinent&nbsp;
in aggro decks, and especially in Voltron decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.320
which typically look to knock players down one&nbsp;
at a time with commander damage. Careful threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.640
assessment and a few swings with a big commander&nbsp;
can be worth countless pieces of removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.880
and for the pieces of removal that do exist&nbsp;
in a deck, it can be beneficial to have them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:20.960
be targeted more toward the stages of the game&nbsp;
that are worth worrying about. Get rid of some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.960
blockers, kill something that can let an opponent&nbsp;
stabilize, remove a bigger mana dork that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.280
let your opponent contest you. Flame Slash isn’t&nbsp;
going to be a great card in most slower decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.360
because it misses a whole lot of stuff you’ll&nbsp;
see in the later stages of a commander game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.840
but that’s not super important if your most&nbsp;
important turns are going to be happening earlier.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.200
Ultimately, all I’m trying to say here is&nbsp;
that different decks will utilize removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.800
for very different purposes. If you’re&nbsp;
playing a creature-based aggressive deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.160
you might want to focus on options that can&nbsp;
clear out blockers, and you likely won’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.400
much in the way of conventional board wipes unless&nbsp;
you have ways to make them one-sided. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.680
playing a combo deck, you’ll want to focus your&nbsp;
interaction on dealing with obstacles to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.560
combo, and spending removal on only the most&nbsp;
imminent of threats. In either of these cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:04.560
your interaction will exist predominantly&nbsp;
to buy yourself time, and things opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.560
have that require time on their end like a&nbsp;
draw or mana engine might not be a concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.960
to you at all. By contrast, if you’re playing&nbsp;
a slower, more controlling deck, you’ll want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.120
to focus more of your removal on knocking out&nbsp;
resource generation such as card draw engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.520
since not letting opponents get ahead of you&nbsp;
will be important in winning in the long game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.680
You also might want to focus on interaction that&nbsp;
targets multiple things or which accomplishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.480
multiple tasks, since those tend to be more&nbsp;
card-efficient. These cards do tend to cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.960
more mana, though, which brings me to the final&nbsp;
little myth I want to tackle. Let’s talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.240
interaction staples of the format, specifically&nbsp;
when they should be played—or not played.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.840
Typically, the forms of removal considered&nbsp;
“staples” are ones that cost very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.640
mana and hit a limited range of targets, such as&nbsp;
Nature’s Claim, or which cost a bit more mana but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.200
which hit a variety of different targets, such as&nbsp;
Generous Gift. There are also a handful of board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.480
wipes that also end up heavily played because&nbsp;
they’re just hands-down the best thing available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:01.200
in the color. Now, am I about to explain to you&nbsp;
why these cards are garbage that you need to rip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.960
out of your deck? No. In fact, I’m about to tell&nbsp;
you exactly why these cards are generally so good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.960
because I think a lot of people have a tendency to&nbsp;
accept that these cards are always the best option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:16.000
and treat it as dogma, a primordial and unshakable&nbsp;
feature of our world alongside gravity and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.720
rising of the sun, and this does this cards a bit&nbsp;
of a disservice. Let’s start with the flexible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:27.040
removal spells—why are Beast Within, Chaos Warp,&nbsp;
and Generous Gift good cards? Right off the bat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:32.800
let’s make something clear—one of these cards I’m&nbsp;
a lot less likely to play than the other two. Can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.920
you guess which one? If you said Generous Gift,&nbsp;
you’re correct, and that’s not an insult to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.400
card itself. It’s functionally identical to Beast&nbsp;
Within, after all, unless your opponent is playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:49.200
an Elephant deck. No, it’s merely a statement&nbsp;
that Beast Within and Chaos Warp are exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.400
cards—they are hard removal in colors that really&nbsp;
don’t have much hard removal for creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.960
and in the case of Chaos Warp, in a color that&nbsp;
lacks enchantment removal on top of that. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.720
are several two color combinations in which&nbsp;
these will be some of the only removal for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.840
certain permanent types you’ll get, and that’s to&nbsp;
say nothing of our monocolored compatriots. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.840
you won’t catch me wasting my breath trying to get&nbsp;
people to take these out of their decks. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:16.400
there are also color combinations where you’ll&nbsp;
have access to lots of other options—Beast Within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.200
won’t feel as essential in a green-black&nbsp;
deck since you’re going to have a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.400
of other reliable ways to remove creatures, and&nbsp;
as I alluded to earlier, any deck that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.880
white is going to have ways to deal with a&nbsp;
variety of permanent types already available.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.320
So, does that mean that these cards aren’t worth&nbsp;
playing in color combinations where they’re not in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.360
some wholly unique niche? Not necessarily. For&nbsp;
one, these hit lands, though the importance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.680
that fact can vary a lot depending on the power&nbsp;
level and matchup you find yourself in. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.840
if your interaction suite is relatively narrow,&nbsp;
somewhere in the ballpark of 8-12 cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.800
there will be a lot of utility in having each of&nbsp;
those cards be as flexible as possible. If I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.840
packing 9 pieces of interaction, after my first&nbsp;
seven draws of the game plus my opening hand, I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.480
on average seen about one and a quarter of these.&nbsp;
If I’m running 14 pieces of interaction, I’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.600
around 2 instead, and if I’m running 18 pieces of&nbsp;
interaction that number is more than 2 and a half.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.600
If I have a diverse interaction suite and I’m&nbsp;
seeing multiple pieces of interaction pretty early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:13.680
into a game, there’s a good chance I’ll have a way&nbsp;
to deal with a specific problem unfolding in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.720
of me, but if that average is lower, running more&nbsp;
of these versatile staples will likely be a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.760
idea. Personally I prefer to run a higher count of&nbsp;
interaction that’s more carefully integrated into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.480
what my deck is doing, but there will be times&nbsp;
when it makes sense to run a lower count—some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.960
decks can threaten player kills or wins faster,&nbsp;
in which case removal won’t be as necessary, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.040
other decks will be tight on deck space and might&nbsp;
struggle to integrate removal into what they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.520
doing. Regardless, versatility of removal is going&nbsp;
to be one of the deckbuilding knobs that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.640
modulate, and it’s good to have an understanding&nbsp;
of when it’s more important and when it’s less.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:52.080
Now let’s get to the cheapest removal out there,&nbsp;
your 0, 1, and 2 mana options. These are powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.560
for a very simple reason—they’re just mana&nbsp;
efficient. If you’re exiling a 4 or 5 mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.800
creature with a Swords to Plowshares, that’s&nbsp;
going to be a significant tempo advantage. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.280
will be most relevant in a fast, proactive deck,&nbsp;
one that is mostly dialed in on its own gameplan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.800
and just needs little bits of removal that it&nbsp;
can fit in around the edges of turns in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:14.640
to prevent opponents from causing problems. The&nbsp;
most extreme cases of this are in cEDH—here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.280
almost all instant-speed interaction is going&nbsp;
to be 0-1 mana, and you’ll sometimes see 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:23.360
mana bounce spells played because it’s just&nbsp;
that worthwhile to temporarily deal with any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.920
nonland permanent for 1 mana. Even if you’re&nbsp;
not playing some insane combo deck trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:32.800
pop off on turn 4, low cost removal options&nbsp;
can still feel quite good if you’re willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.520
to play a lot of card draw. Because that’s the&nbsp;
downside of these—they’re single target removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:42.320
and some of the most popular options actively&nbsp;
give your opponent a thing, so by default you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.720
going to be falling behind the table in cards.&nbsp;
If you’re happy to go ham on card draw in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.120
to counteract this fact, that’s fine enough,&nbsp;
but going for removal that can hit more targets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.840
or which provides some other benefit can really&nbsp;
help a deck to keep up in cards and run smoothly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.960
especially if there’s a lot of elements to&nbsp;
fit into a deck that will be taxing your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:03.840
deck space. If you want a stronger sense of how to&nbsp;
densify a removal suite like this, I’d recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:09.680
watching my removal video from 11 months ago,&nbsp;
because that was a large part of that video.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:14.800
I said earlier that 0-2 mana removal is strong&nbsp;
due to its mana efficiency, and for some use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:19.440
cases that’s actually kind of an understatement.&nbsp;
Specifically, any time a deck is holding up mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:24.320
to use interaction at instant speed, there is&nbsp;
effectively a mana cost associated with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.480
since if the interaction wasn’t needed the mana&nbsp;
goes unspent. This is one reason why cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.720
instant-speed kill spells are popular, and it’s&nbsp;
also why low-cost counterspells are especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:40.240
highly prioritized in EDH. And that’s good and&nbsp;
fine—a deck with mostly sorcery speed plays will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:45.120
want its counterspells as cheaply-costed&nbsp;
as possible—but as with low-cost removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:49.760
the issue comes when players treat this preference&nbsp;
as unshakeable dogma. 3 mana counterspells aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:54.960
inherently bad, nor are 4 mana counterspells or&nbsp;
5 mana counterspells—what matters is whether you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.800
have other things to do with that mana you hold&nbsp;
up. The simplest version of this is in something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.600
like a draw-go style control deck. My friend&nbsp;
Hans has a Lilah, Undefeated Slickshot deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:08.640
that follows a simple premise—there’s 20 blue red&nbsp;
instants that draw cards and/or remove things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:12.160
and once Lilah is on the board the deck&nbsp;
just plays very reactively, mostly only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:16.560
casting plotted spells on its own turn, a thing&nbsp;
that doesn’t require mana. This allows it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:20.800
play a suite of exclusively high-mana-cost&nbsp;
counterspells, which can have backbreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:25.840
upsides compared to their low-cost counterparts.&nbsp;
Aethersnatch fully yoinks an opposing spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.160
Sublime Epiphany does a whole bunch of stuff,&nbsp;
and ending the turn with Time Stop in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.040
to a critical main phase spell or ability&nbsp;
can be equal parts hilarious and devastating.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.040
You might argue that this is niche advice, since&nbsp;
most decks aren’t going to be playing at instant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:43.680
speed, but it’s nonetheless good to keep in mind&nbsp;
as an option. The other way to have higher-costed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.720
options make sense is by having unspent mana&nbsp;
rollover between turns in some way. Storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:53.120
lands or a Kruphix-adjacent commander might&nbsp;
come to mind here, but the most well-supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:57.520
option for this is treasures—you can spend&nbsp;
treasures on counterspells if they’re needed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:01.760
and if they aren't needed the treasures get to&nbsp;
stick around. Finally, there are also a smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:06.000
number of counterspells with some built-in&nbsp;
modality. I’m a big fan of Mystic Confluence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:10.640
and Cryptic Command and Thassa’s Intervention are&nbsp;
other cards that also feel fine enough played for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.120
their other modes. This is also why Three Steps&nbsp;
Ahead has broken the three mana counterspell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:19.440
mold and actually seen decent levels of&nbsp;
play—it can be a three mana counterspell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:23.600
but if it’s not needed it can also be a three&nbsp;
mana filtering spell, and it can also soak up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.480
extra mana with decently mana-efficient results.&nbsp;
There aren’t a ton of amazing modal counterspells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:32.240
at the moment, but this is definitely an&nbsp;
area that I’d keep an eye on, and if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:36.880
see more options like this printed I could see&nbsp;
EDH shifting toward higher-costed counterspells.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.960
As a final note, counterspells are one of the&nbsp;
rare types of card that will consistently embody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:45.440
multiple roles in decks—they’re a form of removal&nbsp;
in a sense since they can nail pivotal spells from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:50.000
opponents, and they’re useful for protection&nbsp;
since they can counter opposing interaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.400
When I’m deckbuilding, how I categorize them will&nbsp;
depend a lot on how important protection is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:59.040
my gameplan. If I expect to have critical plays my&nbsp;
opponents will be trying to answer, I’ll generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.360
either put counterspells in their own category,&nbsp;
or lump them into a protection category along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:07.920
with other sorts of protection cards. Sometimes&nbsp;
I’ll even throw recursion spells into a protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:12.320
category as well if there are critical permanents&nbsp;
to protect, which can help with conceptualizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:15.920
a deck so long as you can keep in mind that&nbsp;
different cards are accomplishing the protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:20.880
task in different ways. On the other hand, if I&nbsp;
have less to protect and I expect my counterspells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:25.360
to be mostly aimed at stopping my opponents’&nbsp;
plays, putting them in their own category is less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:29.520
helpful for understanding my deck, and I’m more&nbsp;
likely to lump them in with the other interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:34.560
in a deck. And, that’s what this is about—how&nbsp;
you categorize your cards matters for being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:38.880
to understand your own deck and make sure you’re&nbsp;
able to accomplish the different tasks you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:43.600
accomplish. My Sharuum Slide deck does use its&nbsp;
seven counterspells for protection sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:47.520
but I keep these cards in the broader control&nbsp;
category because these cards are a bit too messy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.680
in what they accomplish to put into a standalone&nbsp;
category. Three of them are counterspells with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:56.800
cycling that can be used as fuel for Astral Slide,&nbsp;
one also serves as a flickerable removal engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:01.440
one of them has transmute, and how am I even&nbsp;
supposed to categorize what Nimble Obstructionist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:06.160
does? In this case, the interaction suite of this&nbsp;
deck is too convoluted and multifaceted to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:10.880
worth trying to make more digestible, and I know&nbsp;
any overhaul of it is going to require a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:16.560
lot of effort anyway. The one aspect of my control&nbsp;
suite I do differentiate with a subcategory is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.360
creatures with ETBs since building flicker-based&nbsp;
engines to suppress opponents is a key part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:26.880
deck, and if I click the category in Archidekt&nbsp;
to select those cards this becomes evident.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:30.320
I have other decks I could go into detail&nbsp;
discussing the interaction suites of and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:34.880
how I categorize them, but that would pretty&nbsp;
well destroy the pacing of this video. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.320
in preparation for MagicCon Vegas I’m&nbsp;
doing audits of a bunch of my decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:41.760
and so I’m releasing a bonus segment of how the&nbsp;
interaction suites of each of my decks are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:45.760
and what changes I’m looking to make before&nbsp;
Vegas. Some of these decks I haven’t visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:51.120
a whole lot lately, so it’s kinda fun evaluating&nbsp;
the decisions of my past self. To access that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:55.280
you can either join my Patreon or my newly&nbsp;
launched YouTube membership—this video will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:59.920
be both places. With that said, thanks for&nbsp;
watching, and here’s a preview of that video.

